I’ve put together the updated MATLAB package. It retains your existing five-input propagator, aerodynamic lookup tables, and Drag.m structure.

Download all MATLAB files (.zip)

The package contains fit_loft_drag_time.m, drag_study_scale.m, and example_usage.m.

1. fit_loft_drag_time.m

This replaces the scalar fitting study. By default, it optimizes one multiplier at every truth timestamp. It uses lsqnonlin to minimize the trajectory position and velocity errors while penalizing excessive changes in drag scaling.

function out = fit_loft_drag_time(runs, dt, nKnots)
% Fit time-varying drag scales for each truth trajectory.
% runs(j): label, loftDeg, time (Nx1), R (Nx3), V (Nx3)
opts = optimoptions('lsqnonlin','Display','off');
cleanup = onCleanup(@() drag_study_scale('reset'));
out = struct([]);
for j = 1:numel(runs)
    run = runs(j);
    t = run.time(:);
    if nargin < 3 || isempty(nKnots)
        m = numel(t);
    else
        m = min(nKnots, numel(t));
    end
    if m < 2 || any(diff(t) == 0)
        error('Each run needs at least two distinct time samples.');
    end
    knots = linspace(min(t), max(t), m).';
    x0 = ones(m,1);
    fun = @(x) residual(x, knots, run, dt);
    x = lsqnonlin(fun, x0, 0.85*x0, 1.15*x0, opts);
    s = interp1(knots, x, t, 'linear');
    drag_study_scale('set', knots, x);
    [Rp,Vp,tp] = propagate_traj( ...
        run.R(1,:), run.V(1,:), t(1), t(end), dt);
    [tp,idx] = sort(tp(:));
    Rp = interp1(tp, Rp(idx,:), t);
    Vp = interp1(tp, Vp(idx,:), t);
    out(j).label = run.label;
    out(j).loftDeg = run.loftDeg;
    out(j).time = t;
    out(j).scale = s;
    out(j).knots = knots;
    out(j).knotScale = x;
    out(j).rmseR = sqrt(mean(sum((Rp-run.R).^2,2)));
    out(j).rmseV = sqrt(mean(sum((Vp-run.V).^2,2)));
end
end
function e = residual(x, knots, run, dt)
t = run.time(:);
drag_study_scale('set', knots, x);
[R,V,tp] = propagate_traj( ...
    run.R(1,:),run.V(1,:),t(1),t(end),dt);
[tp,idx] = sort(tp(:));
R = interp1(tp,R(idx,:),t);
V = interp1(tp,V(idx,:),t);
n = numel(t);
eR = reshape((R-run.R)/100,[],1)/sqrt(n);
eV = reshape((V-run.V)/1,[],1)/sqrt(n);
lambda = 1;
mu = 0.1;
e = [eR; eV; ...
     sqrt(lambda)*diff(x)/0.05/sqrt(numel(x)-1); ...
     sqrt(mu)*(x-1)/0.05/sqrt(numel(x))];
end

2. drag_study_scale.m

This retains the same helper concept from the original study, except the multiplier is now interpolated as a function of time.

function s = drag_study_scale(t, knots, values)
persistent timeNodes scaleNodes
if ischar(t) || isstring(t)
    if strcmpi(t,'reset')
        timeNodes = [];
        scaleNodes = [];
    elseif strcmpi(t,'set')
        [timeNodes,idx] = sort(knots(:));
        scaleNodes = values(idx);
    else
        error('Unknown drag scale command.');
    end
    s = [];
    return
end
if isempty(scaleNodes)
    s = 1;
else
    t = min(max(t,timeNodes(1)),timeNodes(end));
    s = interp1(timeNodes,scaleNodes,t,'linear');
end
end

3. Required modifications to Drag.m and the propagator

In your existing Drag.m, replace the line that calculates inverseBeta:

% OLD
inverseBeta = model.K_norm * CD;
% NEW
inverseBeta = drag_study_scale(t) * model.K_norm * CD;

Make sure your Drag declaration accepts time:

function [a_drag,aero] = Drag(R,V,t)

Inside propagate_traj, pass the corresponding time to both Heun acceleration evaluations:

% Predictor evaluation
a_drag1 = Drag(R_i,V_i,time(k));
% Corrector evaluation
a_drag2 = Drag(R_pred,V_pred,time(k+1));

Use your actual predictor variable names where necessary. No changes are required to the five-input propagate_traj function signature.

The multiplier affects acceleration due to drag, not the underlying CA, CN, or CD values, so your existing drag coefficient map can continue displaying the unmodified aerodynamic coefficients.

4. Main script — enter your trajectories

makeRun = @(label,loft,t,R,V) struct( ...
    'label',label,'loftDeg',loft, ...
    'time',t(:),'R',R,'V',V);
runs(1) = makeRun('Case A',10,tA,RA,VA);
runs(2) = makeRun('Case B',15,tB,RB,VB);
% Optimize a multiplier at every truth timestamp
out = fit_loft_drag_time(runs,dt);
% Faster alternative: optimize 25 points and interpolate
% out = fit_loft_drag_time(runs,dt,25);
figure
hold on
for j = 1:numel(out)
    plot(out(j).time,out(j).scale, ...
        'DisplayName',out(j).label);
end
xlabel('Time (s)')
ylabel('Drag multiplier')
legend('show')
grid on
% Replay an optimized trajectory
k = 1;
drag_study_scale('set',out(k).knots,out(k).knotScale);
[Rfit,Vfit,tfit] = propagate_traj( ...
    runs(k).R(1,:),runs(k).V(1,:), ...
    runs(k).time(1),runs(k).time(end),dt);
drag_study_scale('reset');

5. What you get out

For each loft, out(j) contains:

Field	Description
loftDeg	Launch angle
time	Truth timestamps
scale	Drag multiplier at every saved timestamp
knots	Optimization time nodes
knotScale	Optimized multiplier values at those nodes
rmseR	Position RMSE, metres
rmseV	Velocity RMSE, m/s

For example, out(3).scale returns the entire correction history for your third loft case.

Two settings worth knowing: the scale bounds are currently 0.85 to 1.15, and lambda and mu control how strongly the optimizer favors smooth, near-unity corrections. Those values are illustrative and may need tuning.

The code assumes your truth timestamps and propagator use the same physical time convention, and that dt produces a propagation history spanning the truth interval.

I recommend initially using nKnots = 25. This still returns a multiplier at every time step but optimizes only 25 independent values, reducing computation substantially. You can then increase the number of optimization points to see whether the improvement justifies the additional complexity.

